<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SEC-1 — backup/transfer exclusion of the encrypted LocalCache.

  WHY AT ALL, given android:allowBackup="false":
  allowBackup="false" switches off cloud backup (Google Drive) and adb backup,
  but on Android 12+ NOT the device-to-device transfer. The official docs
  (https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup) say:

    "On devices from some device manufacturers, specifying
     android:allowBackup="false" disables cloud-based backup and restore
     (such as Google Drive backups) but doesn't disable device-to-device
     transfers for the app."

  In a D2D transfer the SharedPreferences files would travel to the new device
  — the non-exportable AndroidKeyStore key would not. Every cache slot would be
  undecryptable there, and the outbox (which the server CANNOT reconstruct)
  would be destroyed. So we want that state cleanly ABSENT instead of broken:
  after a transfer the app starts like a fresh install, loads from the server
  and creates a new DEK.

  Also from the docs, which is why BOTH sections are spelled out:

    "If there are no rules for a particular backup mode, such as if the
     <device-transfer> section is missing, that mode is fully enabled for all
     content except for no-backup and cache directories"

  What is excluded:
    * domain="sharedpref" path="."
        - shared_prefs/FlutterSharedPreferences.xml  (shared_preferences, i.e.
          LocalCache plus the gotrue session)
        - shared_prefs/FlutterSecureStorage.xml      (flutter_secure_storage,
          the wrapped DEK — worthless without the KeyStore key anyway)
    * domain="file" path="datastore"
        - files/datastore/FlutterSharedPreferences.preferences_pb, the
          DataStore path of the newer shared_preferences Android backends.
          Today SharedPreferences.getInstance() runs over the legacy XML path;
          the exclusion stops a future plugin switch from silently reopening
          the gap.

  What is deliberately NOT excluded (P10-04) — the recipe photos:
  `getApplicationDocumentsDirectory()` maps to context.getDir("flutter"), i.e.
  /data/user/0/com.eatova.app/app_flutter, so recipe_images/<uid>/ sits in
  domain "root", path "app_flutter" (NOT domain "file", which is getFilesDir()).
  It is the only thing the app keeps there. Excluding it would take
  `<exclude domain="root" path="app_flutter" />`; that line is left out on
  purpose:
    * The bytes exist NOWHERE else. Recipe and coach-card photos are never
      uploaded (the row only carries `local:<name>.jpg`), so excluding them
      from the transfer would silently and permanently destroy the user's own
      pictures on every phone switch.
    * A D2D transfer is user-initiated, goes to the user's OWN new device and
      involves no third party — unlike cloud backup, which stays off for
      everything. On iOS these same files ride along in the iCloud device
      backup (PRIVACY.md says so), so carrying them here is the smaller
      exposure, not a new one.
    * Unlike the cache they survive the transfer INTACT: no key is involved,
      and both the `local:` reference and the `<uid>` folder name come back
      from the server, so the photos re-attach to their recipes.
  Accepted residual: if a DIFFERENT account signs in first on the new device,
  the transferred <uid> folder stays behind as orphaned files —
  RecipeImageStore only ever reads the active uid's namespace, so the app can
  never surface them. The same already applies to an iOS restore.
  PRIVACY.md is worded to match: cloud backups excluded entirely, the direct
  device-to-device transfer named as the one path that carries these photos.

  android:fullBackupContent for Android 11 and older is deliberately NOT set:
  there, allowBackup="false" covers local transfer as well.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="datastore" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="datastore" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
